{#
  Arm F — the GENERATION CEILING.

  The official `_oracle` file: evidence-only sessions, nothing else. This is the
  score a reader achieves when retrieval has already done its job perfectly, so
  it bounds everything downstream.

  It is free (no extra API calls beyond the model turn) and it answers the
  question that separates two different failure modes:

    Arm F >> Arm A  →  retrieval is binding. Better retrieval would help.
    Arm F ~= Arm A  →  the WRITE PATH or the READER is binding, and no amount
                      of retrieval tuning will move the number.

  That distinction is worth the run. EvalMem measures retrieval failing 22.1% of
  the time against encoding at 7.7% and generation at 6.5% — if our own write
  path reproduces a similar split, the K-curve is dominated by a K-independent
  constant and must be reported as one rather than as a saturation effect.

  Unlike Arm E, this prompt DIFFERS from Arm A's: it carries no distractor
  context at all, which is the entire point of the condition.
#}
system: |
  You are answering a question about a conversation history. You are given the
  exact sessions that contain the answer and nothing else. Answer directly.

user: |
  {{ memories }}

  Question: {{ question }}

  Answer with the shortest span that answers the question. Do not explain.

source:
  file: lme_oracle.json
  # Measured in P1: 500 records, mean 1.9 sessions per question (min 1, max 6),
  # against 47.7 on the S split. That 25x reduction in distractor context is the
  # whole treatment.
  expected_sessions_per_question: 1.9
